Relevant concepts: Symmetry Groups; Symmetry Group of Square
Proof blueprint: Recall that a [[Definition:Submagma|submagma]] of an [[Definition:Algebraic Structure|algebraic structure]] $\mathcal S$ is a [[Definition:Subset|subsets]] of $\mathcal S$ which is [[Definition:Closed Algebraic Structure|closed]].
Let $\mathcal X$ be the [[Definition:Set|set]] of all [[Definition:Submagma|submagmas]] of $\mathcal S$.
From [[Empty Set is Submagma of Magma]]:
:$\O \in \mathcal X$
From [[Magma is Submagma of Itself]]:
:$\mathcal S \in \mathcal X$
From [[Idempotent Magma Element forms Singleton Submagma]]:
:$\set e \in \mathcal X$
Let us refer to the [[Definition:Cayley Table|Cayley table]]:
=== [[Symmetry Group of Square/Cayley Table|Cayley Table of Symmetry Group of Square]] ===
{{:Symmetry Group of Square/Cayley Table}}
Taking each of the elements of $\mathcal S$ in order:
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn | l = t_x \circ t^x
      | r = e
}}
{{eqn | l = t_y \circ t^y
      | r = e
}}
{{eqn | l = t_{AC} \circ t^{AC}
      | r = e
}}
{{eqn | l = t_{BD} \circ t^{BD}
      | r = e
}}
{{eqn | l = r^2 \circ r^2
      | r = e
}}
{{eqn | l = r \circ r
      | r = r^2
}}
{{eqn | l = r \circ r^2
      | r = r^3
}}
{{eqn | l = r \circ r^3
      | r = e
}}
{{eqn | l = r^3 \circ r^3
      | r = r^2
}}
{{eqn | l = r^3 \circ r^2
      | r = r
}}
{{eqn | l = r^3 \circ r
      | r = e
}}
{{end-eqn}}
Thus we have:
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn | l = \set {e, r^2}
      | o = \in
      | r = \mathcal X
}}
{{eqn | l = \set {e, t_x}
      | o = \in
      | r = \mathcal X
}}
{{eqn | l = \set {e, t_y}
      | o = \in
      | r = \mathcal X
}}
{{eqn | l = \set {e, t_{AC} }
      | o = \in
      | r = \mathcal X
}}
{{eqn | l = \set {e, t_{BD} }
      | o = \in
      | r = \mathcal X
}}
{{eqn | l = \set {e, r, r^2, r^3}
      | o = \in
      | r = \mathcal X
}}
{{end-eqn}}
Next note by inspection that:
:$\set {e, r^2, t_x, t_y} \in \mathcal X$
and:
:$\set {e, r^2, t_{AC}, t_{BD} } \in \mathcal X$
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn | l = \set {e, r^2, t_x, t_y}
      | o = \in
      | r = \mathcal X
}}
{{eqn | l = \set {e, r^2, t_{AC}, t_{BD} }
      | o = \in
      | r = \mathcal X
}}
{{end-eqn}}
Finally note by inspection that:
: any [[Definition:Closed Algebraic Structure|closed]] [[Definition:Subset|subset]] of $\mathcal S$ which contains both $r$ and any of the reflections contains all the elements of $\mathcal S$
: any [[Definition:Closed Algebraic Structure|closed]] [[Definition:Subset|subset]] of $\mathcal S$ which contains both $r^3$ and any of the reflections contains all the elements of $\mathcal S$.
Thus there are no more [[Definition:Proper Subset|proper subsets]] of $\mathcal S$ which are [[Definition:Submagma|submagmas]] of $\mathcal S$.
{{qed}}

